# the argument of kant-practical-4.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
atom	under_discipline	we stand under a discipline of reason
atom	love_suitable	doing good from love and sympathetic good will, like volunteers above the thought of duty, is the true moral maxim of our conduct suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men
atom	love_in_law	the maxim of doing good from love and our own good pleasure sets the determining principle of our will in the law itself and in respect for this law
atom	legislative	we are legislative members of a moral kingdom rendered possible by freedom
atom	subjects	we are subjects in the moral kingdom, not the sovereign
atom	reject_authority	we mistake our inferior position as creatures and presumptuously reject the authority of the moral law
atom	revolt	we revolt from the moral law in spirit, even though its letter is fulfilled
atom	duty_only	duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law
P1	S2	under_discipline
P2	S2	under_discipline → (love_suitable → love_in_law)
P3	S1	¬love_in_law
P4	S4	legislative ∧ subjects
P5	S4	subjects ∧ reject_authority → revolt
P6	S3	duty_only	kind normative
# S1's "this is not yet the true moral maxim" taken as the main conclusion; S2 supplies the requirement that the will be determined by the law alone
C	S1	¬love_suitable	from P1 P2 P3
